   $q$Read the sentence: "Ravi studied the map carefully, he still took a wrong turn at the fork." Which revision fixes the comma splice and shows a contrast?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Although Ravi studied the map carefully, he still took a wrong turn at the fork.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Although Ravi studied the map carefully. He still took a wrong turn at the fork.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subordinate-clause-fragment"},{"text":"Ravi studied the map carefully, however, he still took a wrong turn at the fork.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-transition"},{"text":"Ravi studied the map carefully, so he still took a wrong turn at the fork.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-conjunction-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Although Ravi studied the map carefully, he still took a wrong turn at the fork.$q$, $q$"Although" signals a contrast and turns the first idea into a dependent clause joined by a comma. "However" with commas on both sides is still a comma splice, because "however" is a transition and needs a semicolon before it.$q$, 'draft', null)
